export const childrenRecommendations = [
  {label:"Best Stroller",recommendations:[
    {productName:"Stokke YOYO³",productHref:"https://www.stokke.com/USA/en-us/strollers/yoyo3",note:"The YOYO³ is the city stroller as folding bicycle: compact, quick, and designed around the reality of stairs, cafés, taxis and narrow apartment halls. It does not try to become a rolling nursery. It folds into something civilized, steers with one hand and keeps the parent from feeling as if every outing requires logistics staff. The best children’s gear should reduce the temperature of the day. This one does that with unusual grace."},
    {productName:"Bugaboo Butterfly 2",productHref:"https://www.bugaboo.com/us-en/strollers/bugaboo-butterfly-2/",note:"Bugaboo’s Butterfly 2 is the polished travel answer: small when folded, substantial when open and more comfortable than many strollers that advertise portability as their only virtue. The basket is useful, the ride feels grown-up and the mechanism avoids the sense that a parent is being asked to solve a puzzle in public. It is the stroller for families who move through airports, sidewalks and weekends with the same child and different expectations."},
    {productName:"UPPAbaby MINU V3",productHref:"https://uppababy.com/strollers/lightweight/minu/",note:"The MINU V3 has the reassuring vocabulary of UPPAbaby — good materials, calm colors, a sensible fold — compressed into something easier to live with. It is not the smallest stroller in the world, which is fine; the trade is a little more presence for a little more comfort. For families who want one compact stroller that still feels like equipment rather than compromise, the MINU makes a persuasive case."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best High Chair",recommendations:[
    {productName:"Stokke Tripp Trapp",productHref:"https://www.stokke.com/USA/en-us/highchairs/tripp-trapp",note:"The Tripp Trapp remains the high chair because it behaves like furniture rather than a plastic interruption. Peter Opsvik’s design brings the child to the table, which is the whole idea, and then keeps adjusting as the child grows. Its genius is not nostalgia but continuity: one chair through years of meals, homework, snacks and crumbs. Children’s objects often look temporary. This one has the good manners to stay."},
    {productName:"Bugaboo Giraffe",productHref:"https://www.bugaboo.com/us-en/high-chair/bugaboo-giraffe/",note:"Bugaboo’s Giraffe is the cleaner, more contemporary high-chair proposition. It adjusts quickly, looks light in a room and avoids the gear-room feeling that often follows children into the dining area. The chair has a little less heirloom romance than the Tripp Trapp but more immediate ease. It suits homes where the kitchen is also the living room and every object has to justify being visible."},
    {productName:"Lalo The Chair",productHref:"https://www.meetlalo.com/products/the-chair",note:"Lalo’s chair is friendly without becoming cute. The proportions are soft, the colors are considered and the conversion from high chair to play chair gives it a life beyond purées and thrown peas. It is not trying to be Scandinavian canon; it is trying to be useful in a young household that still cares how the room looks. That is a modest but meaningful achievement."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Children’s Book",recommendations:[
    {productName:"The Snowy Day",productHref:"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/334893/the-snowy-day-by-ezra-jack-keats/",note:"Ezra Jack Keats’s The Snowy Day is a small masterpiece of quiet observation. The plot is almost nothing, which is why it lasts: a child walks, notices, pockets snow and learns how the world changes underfoot. The collage is warm without sentimentality, and the book trusts children to understand atmosphere. It is a bedtime object, yes, but also a lesson in looking closely at ordinary weather."},
    {productName:"Where the Wild Things Are",productHref:"https://www.harpercollins.com/products/where-the-wild-things-are-maurice-sendak",note:"Sendak’s classic earns its shelf space because it gives children both mischief and return. The wild rumpus is not a moral failure; it is imagination doing its work before supper reappears. The drawings still have a strange authority, and the text wastes nothing. Many children’s books explain too much. This one lets a child be furious, powerful, loved and hungry in fewer pages than most books manage an introduction."},
    {productName:"Last Stop on Market Street",productHref:"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/316495/last-stop-on-market-street-by-matt-de-la-pena-illustrated-by-christian-robinson/",note:"Last Stop on Market Street is a generous city book: buses, rain, questions, music and a grandmother quietly expanding a child’s sense of what counts as beautiful. Christian Robinson’s illustrations have the clarity of good public signage and the warmth of a Sunday errand. The book does not preach attention, but it teaches it anyway. That is the trick: civic life made legible to a child."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Toy",recommendations:[
    {productName:"Grimm’s Large Rainbow",productHref:"https://www.grimms.eu/en/products/building-and-stacking/large-rainbow",note:"Grimm’s Large Rainbow is the rare toy that becomes more useful because it refuses to specify the game. It can be tunnel, bridge, landscape, sculpture or obstacle, depending on the hour and the child. The colors are cheerful without plastic glare, and the wood ages into the room rather than out of it. It is expensive for a toy, but cheap for an object that might survive several imaginations."},
    {productName:"LEGO Classic Large Creative Brick Box",productHref:"https://www.lego.com/en-us/product/large-creative-brick-box-10698",note:"LEGO’s Classic brick box is the democratic answer to childhood construction. No franchise, no prescribed universe, just enough color and possibility to make a floor disappear under invention. It encourages the best kind of mess: iterative, spatial, stubborn and occasionally brilliant. Many toys entertain a child. Bricks recruit a child into authorship, which is better."},
    {productName:"MAGNA-TILES Classic 100-Piece Set",productHref:"https://www.magnatiles.com/products/classic-100-piece-set",note:"MAGNA-TILES have the immediate satisfaction of a good interface. The pieces snap, stand, glow near a window and turn geometry into something children can feel with their hands. They are excellent for collaborative play because the structure is always growing, falling or being reconsidered. The best educational toys do not announce their educational purpose. They just make the child keep building."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Kids Backpack",recommendations:[
    {productName:"Fjällräven Kånken Mini",productHref:"https://www.fjallraven.com/us/en-us/bags-gear/kanken/kanken-bags/kanken-mini/",note:"The Kånken Mini has become a small icon because it respects both child and object. It is light, square, easy to pack and serious enough not to look like a seasonal cartoon. The colors carry personality without surrendering to novelty, and the shape makes lunch, books and treasures visible rather than swallowed. It is a child’s bag with adult design discipline, which is exactly why it works."},
    {productName:"Patagonia Refugito Day Pack 12L",productHref:"https://www.patagonia.com/product/kids-refugito-day-pack-12-liters/47890.html",note:"Patagonia’s Refugito is the outdoor-child backpack that still makes sense at school. The fabric is tough, the straps are comfortable and the scale is right for smaller bodies that should not look overburdened by their own supplies. It is useful for classrooms, hikes and grandparents’ houses, which is the real test. Children’s gear should survive both mud and markers."},
    {productName:"STATE Kane Kids Backpack",productHref:"https://statebags.com/products/kane-kids-backpack",note:"STATE’s Kane backpack is a neater, more urban choice: clean panels, good colors and enough organization to keep school life from becoming a soft avalanche. It feels less outdoorsy than Patagonia and less iconic than Fjällräven, but more polished for a city routine. For children who already have strong color opinions, it offers taste without making the parent surrender entirely."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Lunchbox",recommendations:[
    {productName:"PlanetBox Rover",productHref:"https://www.planetbox.com/products/rover-lunchbox",note:"The PlanetBox Rover turns lunch into a tidy little composition. Stainless steel compartments keep foods visible and separate, which matters to children who regard a damp sandwich as a personal betrayal. It is durable, dishwasher-friendly and satisfyingly analog. The price is not small, but neither is the pile of plastic containers it can replace. A good lunchbox should make packing easier and lunch less sad."},
    {productName:"Bentgo Kids",productHref:"https://bentgo.com/products/bentgo-kids-prints",note:"Bentgo Kids is the practical bento for families who need leak resistance, bright colors and a container that can survive the backpack commute. The compartments encourage variety without requiring a Pinterest personality, and the latch is manageable for small hands. It is not as elegant as steel, but it is extremely competent. In weekday parenting, competence is often the luxury."},
    {productName:"OmieBox",productHref:"https://www.omielife.com/products/omiebox",note:"OmieBox earns its place by solving the hot-and-cold lunch problem in one object. The thermos insert makes leftovers, noodles or soup feel possible without requiring a second container economy. It is bulkier than simpler boxes, but the flexibility is real. For children bored by cold lunches and parents tired of negotiating with separate jars, it adds range to the school morning."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Balance Bike",recommendations:[
    {productName:"woom GO 1",productHref:"https://woom.com/en_US/products/go-1-toddler-balance-bike",note:"The woom GO 1 treats a child’s first bicycle as a bicycle, not a toy with wheels. It is exceptionally light, properly proportioned and fitted with a hand brake that introduces the habits of real riding without making the lesson feel technical. The frame is quiet, the colors are good and the whole object can be carried home under one arm when enthusiasm runs out. That final detail matters more than most specifications."},
    {productName:"Early Rider Big Foot 12",productHref:"https://earlyrider.com/products/big-foot-12",note:"Early Rider’s Big Foot 12 is the small off-road machine: aluminum frame, pneumatic tires and enough composure for gravel, grass and the uneven edges of a park. It looks like equipment rather than nursery gear, which gives it unusual longevity in both use and appearance. The larger tires add weight beside the woom, but they also add confidence when the pavement ends."},
    {productName:"Strider 12 Sport",productHref:"https://striderbikes.com/buy/shop-all/balance-bikes/12-sport/",note:"The Strider 12 Sport is the democratic original: adjustable, durable and understood by nearly every child who encounters one. Its foam tires are less supple than pneumatic alternatives and there is no brake, but the very low saddle and broad fit range make it unusually easy to share, hand down and keep in motion. Good children’s design should survive several owners. The Strider expects to."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Raincoat",recommendations:[
    {productName:"Fairechild Midi Rain Coat",productHref:"https://fairechild.com/collections/childrens-raincoats",note:"Fairechild makes the rare children’s raincoat that is serious about both weather and appearance. The Midi is cut cleanly, made in Canada from recycled waterproof material and sized with enough room to last beyond one brief season. Its colors belong to forests, clay and wet pavement rather than cartoons. It is the coat for a child who should be allowed into the rain without turning the hallway into an outdoor shop."},
    {productName:"Polarn O. Pyret Stormy Shell Jacket",productHref:"https://www.polarnopyret.com/en/uk/kids-clothes/outerwear/rainwear/raincoats",note:"Polarn O. Pyret’s Stormy shell applies Swedish pragmatism to bad weather: properly waterproof, breathable, visible when necessary and designed for layering rather than bulk. It is less romantic than a waxed fisherman’s coat but more capable across a school year. The jacket is made for children who go outside because it is raining, not despite it."},
    {productName:"Petit Bateau Iconic Raincoat",productHref:"https://www.petit-bateau.us/",note:"Petit Bateau’s yellow raincoat remains the cheerful French answer. The waxed exterior, striped lining and uncomplicated shape have enough identity to feel special without becoming costume. It is not the most technical coat here, but it understands that children’s clothing can carry a little civic brightness. On a gray walk to school, yellow is a useful feature."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Wooden Blocks",recommendations:[
    {productName:"KAPLA 200 Box",productHref:"https://www.kapla.com/en/kapla-200-box_p37.html",note:"KAPLA reduces construction to one beautifully judged piece of pine repeated 200 times. There are no connectors, characters or instructions that must be obeyed; balance is the mechanism and imagination supplies the architecture. The uniform planks allow children to build with surprising precision, then enjoy the equally important act of knocking everything down. It is a French-Dutch classic with the calm authority of a good studio tool."},
    {productName:"Naef Bauhaus Building Game",productHref:"https://www.naefspiele.ch/en/product/bauhaus-bauspiel/",note:"Naef’s Bauhaus building game turns Alma Siedhoff-Buscher’s 1923 design into a compact lesson in color, proportion and possibility. The pieces become boats, furniture, landscapes and small modernist cities without ever losing their sculptural presence. It is expensive, but it is also one of the few toys that can sit on a shelf for decades and become more convincing with age."},
    {productName:"Grimm’s Large Rainbow",productHref:"https://www.grimms.eu/en/products/building-and-stacking/large-rainbow",note:"Grimm’s Large Rainbow is less a block set than a piece of domestic landscape. Its alder arches become tunnels, cradles, walls and improbable balancing structures, while the hand-finished color gives the object warmth without plastic shine. The open form invites a younger child immediately and remains useful as play becomes more elaborate. Few toys manage to be this recognizable and this unspecific at once."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Toy Car",recommendations:[
    {productName:"Playforever Leadbelly",productHref:"https://playforever.us/collections/leadbelly",note:"Playforever’s Leadbelly has the stance of a 1960s touring car reduced to a few confident lines. The rubber tires roll properly, the body feels substantial and the design avoids both realism-by-sticker and the disposable brightness of ordinary toy cars. It is satisfying in a child’s hand and handsome on an adult’s shelf, which is not a compromise so much as evidence that the form was resolved."},
    {productName:"Candylab Americana",productHref:"https://candylabtoys.com/collections/americana",note:"Candylab’s Americana cars turn mid-century American optimism into compact beechwood objects: wagons, taxis, vans and racers with just enough detail to establish a character. They are robust, graphic and especially good when several gather into an accidental streetscape. The line understands that a toy car is partly transportation and partly architecture."},
    {productName:"Vilac Wooden Racing Car",productHref:"https://www.vilac.com/en/",note:"Vilac’s wooden racing cars carry the friendly economy of French toy design: lacquered wood, simple wheels and a silhouette that communicates speed without needing a franchise. They are made to be pushed across floors rather than preserved in packaging. The best one is whichever color the child chooses first; the design is strong enough to survive preference."},
  ]},
  {label:"Best Children’s Chair",recommendations:[
    {productName:"Vitra Panton Junior",productHref:"https://www.vitra.com/en-us/product/details/panton-junior",note:"The Panton Junior is not an adult icon carelessly shrunk. Verner Panton had considered a child’s version from the beginning, and the smaller chair preserves the cantilevered confidence while making it light enough to move between table, bedroom and improvised fort. It gives a child genuine modern furniture rather than a temporary substitute. The chair’s color and silhouette do the decorating by themselves."},
    {productName:"Artek Children’s Chair N65",productHref:"https://www.artek.fi/en/products/childrens-chair-n65",note:"Alvar Aalto’s N65 is the small wooden chair as civic infrastructure. The low back becomes a handle, the bent birch legs are durable and the proportions feel equally at home in a kitchen, library or kindergarten. It does not announce childhood through decoration. It simply gives a smaller person a well-made place to sit, which is the more respectful gesture."},
    {productName:"Nofred Mouse Chair",productHref:"https://nofred.com/products/mouse-chair",note:"Nofred’s Mouse Chair adds just enough character to a clean plywood form. The ear-like back gives the chair a friendly identity, while the broad seat and compact footprint keep it useful for drawing, reading and the serious administrative work of childhood. It is playful without making the room childish, a distinction many children’s furniture brands never learn."},
  ]},
] as const;

